 ### [Hybrid Kd Tree Traversal](20234-hybrid-kd-tree-traversal.md)
-Hybrid Kd Tree Traversal is a method for efficiently accessing heterogeneous volume data by organizing distinct brick types (e.g., dense grids, octrees, voxel lists) into a hierarchical Kd tree structure. It solves the problem of sparse or mixed-format volumes where a single uniform data structure would waste memory or require complex type-checking during ray traversal. Unlike its sibling Adaptive Kd Tree, which adapts subdivision based on data density, this method groups bricks by their storage format to enable specialized, type-specific access routines at the leaves.
+Hybrid Kd Tree Traversal is a method for efficiently accessing heterogeneous volume data by organizing distinct brick types (e.g., dense grids, octrees, voxel lists) into a hierarchical Kd tree structure. It solves the problem of sparse or mixed-format volumes where a single uniform data structure would waste memory or require complex type-checking during ray traversal. Unlike its sibling Adaptive Kd Tree, which adapts subdivision based on data density, this method groups bricks by their storage format to enable specialized, type-specific access routines at the leaves. Choose it when rendering mixed-format volumes to avoid type-checking overhead.
 
 ### [Adaptive Kd Tree](20471-adaptive-kd-tree.md)
-The Adaptive Kd Tree is a hierarchical acceleration structure for volume rendering that stores data values exclusively in the leaves of the tree. It addresses the problem of efficient ray traversal through sparse volumetric data by allowing subdivision in all directions, offering more flexibility than octrees which are restricted to center-based subdivisions. It is distinguished from its sibling, the Hybrid Kd Tree, by its specific leaf-only storage strategy and construction method.
+The Adaptive Kd Tree is a hierarchical acceleration structure for volume rendering that stores data values exclusively in the leaves of the tree. It addresses the problem of efficient ray traversal through sparse volumetric data by allowing subdivision in all directions, offering more flexibility than octrees which are restricted to center-based subdivisions. It is distinguished from its sibling, the Hybrid Kd Tree, by its specific leaf-only storage strategy and construction method. Choose it when data density varies significantly and leaf-only storage reduces memory bandwidth.
 
